---
description: A collector may only be added for a registry source whose terms permit automated collection
alwaysApply: false
globs: "backend/src/collectors/**/*.ts,backend/src/source-registry/**/*.ts,shared/src/sourceRegistry*.ts,shared/src/permission.ts"
---

- Every data source has an entry in the source registry (`shared/src/sourceRegistrySeed.ts`, mirrored in `evidence.source`) with its permission status, terms address, date checked, retention rule, rate and cost limits and independence group.
- An automated collector may only exist and only be enabled for a source whose status is `officialDataset`, `officialApiPermitted`, or `writtenPermissionGranted` with a recorded reference to the written permission. The application (`decideCollectorEnablement`) and a database check constraint both enforce this.
- Sources that are `writtenPermissionRequired`, `prohibited`, `restricted` or `defunct` stay in the registry with their limitations. Do not implement a collector for them because the technology makes it possible. Do not add evasion (proxy rotation, CAPTCHA bypass, disguised clients).
- `restricted` sources (for example the Google Places API) may not store content beyond what their terms allow and may not be combined with non-Google maps.
- Manual and first-party evidence is always allowed, but each observation records its `permissionBasis`. Observations without an acceptable basis are shown but excluded from calculations (`evaluateObservationEligibility`).
- Adding a collector needs: a registry entry checked against the live terms, rate and cost limits, a fixture-based parser test, audit counters, and failure isolation.
